{"name":"Tombstone Grey","hex":"#cec5b6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tombstone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A grave marker, a stone slab or similar object marking a person's grave.","The symbol \"∎\" marking the end of a proof."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To take part in tombstoning: to jump into the sea, etc. from a cliff or other high point so as to enter the water vertically straight.","For a surfboard to stand upright half-submerged in the water (like a tombstone, above) because the surfer is underwater with his or her legrope pulled tight. Often this indicates a surfer in difficulty, either held down by the power of a wave or unconscious and unable to get to the surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tombstone"},{"term":"grey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray.","Synonym of coloured (pertaining to the mixed race of black and white)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gravestone","blocks":[{"text":"A gravestone or tombstone is a marker, usually made of stone, that is placed over a grave. A marker set at the head of the grave may be called a headstone. An especially old or elaborate stone slab may be called a funeral stele, stela, or slab. The use of such markers is traditional for Chinese, Jewish, Christian, and Islamic burials, as well as other traditions. In East Asia, the tomb's spirit tablet is the focus for ancestral veneration and may be removable for greater protection between rituals. Ancient grave markers typically incorporated funerary art, especially details in stone relief. With greater literacy, markers increasingly included inscriptions of the deceased's name, date of birth, and date of death, often along with a personal message or prayer. The presence of a frame for photographs of the deceased is also increasingly common."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Gravestone_of_Andrew_Drake.jpg/330px-Gravestone_of_Andrew_Drake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravestone","wikidata":"Q203443"},{"title":"Grey","blocks":[{"text":"Grey or gray is an intermediate color between black and white. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of lead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg/330px-Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey","wikidata":"Q42519"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/P._Ruotsalainen_grave_1.jpg/500px-P._Ruotsalainen_grave_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/P._Ruotsalainen_grave_1.jpg/1280px-P._Ruotsalainen_grave_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/P._Ruotsalainen_grave_1.jpg/500px-P._Ruotsalainen_grave_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A tombstone at the grave of Paavo Ruotsalainen (1777–1852) in Nilsiä, Kuopio, Finland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AP._Ruotsalainen_grave_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Maym%C5%ABnah_Stone%2C_Gozo_Museum_of_Archaeology%2C_Victoria%2C_Gozo_001.jpg/250px-Maym%C5%ABnah_Stone%2C_Gozo_Museum_of_Archaeology%2C_Victoria%2C_Gozo_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Maym%C5%ABnah_Stone%2C_Gozo_Museum_of_Archaeology%2C